request.prompt:
You are an independent evaluation judge. Grade the proposed remedy, not the
writing style of this prompt. A good remedy changes a concrete future behavior,
is broad enough to cover the failure mode without becoming a platitude, uses a
real `Use when ...` trigger when it is a skill, leaks no evidence-specific
project nouns into the remedy text, and uses the cheapest adequate remedy type.

Score every dimension from 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent). Give exactly one concise
sentence of justification for each score. Do not infer facts that are absent.

PROPOSAL:
{
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "dedup": [
    {
      "existing": "R1",
      "reason": "R1 (fake-verify-before-claim) addresses verifying completion claims before reporting them done. This incident is about delivering unrequested extra scope (a demo beyond the requested single file), not about unverified completion claims.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R2",
      "reason": "R2 is a claude-md rule requiring running a fake release's declared verification command before claiming completion. Unrelated to over-delivering scope on a simple file request.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R3",
      "reason": "R3's own dedup notes describe it as covering a destructive delete performed in violation of a standing user constraint — a different failure (destructive action vs. a standing prohibition) from this incident's over-delivery of unrequested scope on a narrow file request.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "incident_id": 6,
      "quote": "That's not what I asked; the request was a one-file note."
    }
  ],
  "failure_statement": "When a user specifies a narrow, explicit deliverable (e.g. \"just a one-file note\"), the assistant sometimes delivers additional, unrequested artifacts (e.g. extra demo files/scaffolding), causing the user to have to correct scope after the fact.",
  "remedy_content": {
    "target": "global",
    "text": "# Match Delivery to Requested Scope\n\nWhen the user specifies a narrow, explicit deliverable (e.g. \"just one file,\" \"a single note\"), deliver exactly that. Do not add extra files, demos, or scaffolding beyond what was asked — if broader scope seems valuable, propose it separately rather than including it unasked."
  },
  "remedy_type": "claude-md",
  "routing_rationale": "Single incident: user asked for a narrowly-scoped deliverable (\"a one-file note\") and the assistant delivered more than requested, drawing an explicit correction. This is a plain scope-deviation failure with no mechanical signal a hook could check (scope match requires judgment, not a lint rule), and it's not procedural/multi-step enough to warrant a skill — it's a one-line standing discipline (\"match delivery to requested scope\") that fits the cheapest tier, claude-md. No existing remedy (verification-before-claiming-done, or a destructive-delete constraint) covers scope-matching, so this is a new addition rather than a revision.",
  "singleton": true
}
